  "description_html": "Size comparison of the polycotylid plesiosaur <i>Polycotylus latipinnis</i>. The diver silhoutte is from <a href=\"//commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Scuba33.jpg\" title=\"File:Scuba33.jpg\">File:Scuba33.jpg</a>, which is by NOAA and in the public domain.\n<div class=\"mw-heading mw-heading4\"><h4 id=\"References\">References</h4></div>\n<ul><li><cite style=\"font-style:normal\">O'Keefe, F. R.; Chiappe, L. M. (2011). \"<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Luis-Chiappe/publication/51566068_Viviparity_and_K-Selected_Life_History_in_a_Mesozoic_Marine_Plesiosaur_Reptilia_Sauropterygia/links/56267acc08ae4d9e5c4d2bb8/Viviparity-and-K-Selected-Life-History-in-a-Mesozoic-Marine-Plesiosaur-Reptilia-Sauropterygia.pdf\">Viviparity and K-selected life history in a Mesozoic marine plesiosaur (Reptilia, Sauropterygia)</a>\". <i>Science</i> <b>333</b> (6044): 870-873.</cite></li>\n<li><cite style=\"font-style:normal\">Schumacher, B. A.; Martin, J. E. (2016). \"<i>Polycotylus latipinnis</i> Cope (Plesiosauria, Polycotylidae), a nearly complete skeleton from the Niobrara Formation(early Campanian) of southwestern South Dakota\". <i>Journal of Vertebrate Paleontology</i> <b>36</b> (1): e1031341.</cite></li></ul>",
